.TH RS485_EMULATOR 1 "September 2026" "wRS485_emulator" "BUS emulator daemon"

.SH NAME
RS485_emulator - virtual RS485 bus emulator for development and testing

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B RS485_emulator \fI[--foreground] [--help] [--version]\fP

.SH DESCRIPTION
.B RS485_emulator
creates a virtual RS485 bus that can be used to develop and test software without requiring a physical RS485 network.

The emulator provides one virtual master port and a set of virtual slave ports. Applications connected to these ports
can use them as normal serial devices. Like on a real RS485 half-duplex bus, data sent by the bus master are received
by all slaves. However, when multiple slaves try to transmit data at the same time, the behavior of the real and virtual
buses differs. On a real bus, this would typically result in data corruption; in the virtual environment, the conflict
is detected and reported through a log message.

.TP
.B Broken bus simulation
From the 1.0.1 release a new feature allows you to simulate a broken bus's wire event. To generate the event, send a
\fBSIGUSR1\fP UNIX signal to the bus emulator process. It will log the new status: broken. Sending another SIGUSR1
signal you will be able to fix the broken wire, virtually.

.SH OPTIONS
.IP "--foreground"
The process will run in foreground and the log messages will be printed on screen instead of being sent to syslog daemon

.IP "--help"
A usage message will be printed

.IP "--version"
The project's version will be printed

.SH FILES

.IP "/etc/RS485_emulator.cfg"
The configuration file

.IP "/var/run/RS485_emulator.pid"
The PID-file when the process is running as daemon

.IP "/var/lib/RS485_emulator.db"
The SQLite3 db-file

.SH TEST
In the source tree you can find many tests. In order to get a functionality overview, I suggest you to build the fakeMaster1
and fakeSerialDev1 executable files and follow the next steps:
.IP "1)" 4
Open a shell and run the bus emulator executing the following command: <path>/RS485_emulator --foreground
.IP "2)" 4
Open many other shells, and in each of them, execute <path>/fakeSerialDev1 --repo=/tmp/repo-$(date +%s)
.IP "3)" 4
Open yet another shell and execute <path>/fakeMaster1
.PP
You will see the data generated by fakeMaster1 process are received by all slaves fake-devices.

